<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=combine-harvester">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Combine harvester</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1835">1835</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as combine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The combine harvester is a machine that joins reaping, threshing or winnowing and gathering in a single process. In 1835 Hiram Moore built and patented in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the first combine harvester, able to reap, thresh and winnow grain; it was pulled by 20 horses, and by 1839 it had harvested over 20 hectares. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> a stripper was developed by 1843, and Hugh Victor McKay's Sunshine Header-Harvester of 1885 was commercially successful. Self-propelled machines followed from 1911, tractor-drawn ones after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and rotary combines in 1975. It significantly reduced the share of the population in agriculture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/combine-harvester.jpg" alt="Combine harvester" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wikideas1 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corn_combine_harvest_with_grain_cart-4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
